# `#540` PASS 2 -- the dedicated adversarial job: find the smallest edit that re-opens the defect and passes all 22 rows

    VERDICT: SHIP | SHIP-WITH-CHANGES | DO-NOT-SHIP -- <one line naming the deciding item>

**Put the verdict line first.**  The tree under review is `GXEMUL-SEC/` (the working-tree `dev_lca.c`, which carries
the #540 fix).  Ignore any other `src/` under the project root: those copies are pristine 2021 upstream.

**Read ONLY these files. Do not browse anything else:**

    GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_lca.c                 DEVICE_ACCESS(lca_isa), ~275-320 (the fix: two `CACHE_NONE |
                                                     PHYSICAL' forwards)
    GXEMUL-SEC/src/cpus/memory_rw.c                  what PHYSICAL, CACHE_NONE and NO_EXCEPTIONS change
    GXEMUL-SEC/regress/lca_isaphys_probe.py          the 22-row detector: its header (contract) and its rows
    GXEMUL-SEC/regress/artifacts/lcaisaphys/killtable_20260918/PRETABLE.out   the 15 variants already measured

**The defect it fixes.**  The two nested `cpu->memory_rw(... LCA_ISA_BASE + port ...)` forwards passed `CACHE_NONE`
without `PHYSICAL`, so the physical ISA address was translated as a VIRTUAL one: correct only while ptbr == 0 in
kernel mode.  After the guest armed a page table, every ISA access missed (a false zero, or a dropped store).

**What is already measured (16 legs, all killed or disclosed as predicted):** both-reverted, read-only, write-only,
NO_EXCEPTIONS added, bare PHYSICAL (equivalent), PHYSICAL only for ports < 0x200 / >= 0x200, PHYSICAL only at
ofs == 0, PHYSICAL except at ofs == k (k = 1, 2, 3), read arm / write arm at ofs == 0 only, PHYSICAL only when
len == 1, and a K0SEG-alias address form (escapes; disclosed, because the probe never leaves kernel mode).

## TASK 1 -- THE SMALLEST ESCAPE

Name one or more ONE-EDIT variants of the fixed `DEVICE_ACCESS(lca_isa)` that (a) re-introduce a wrong address class
(or a dropped/zeroed access) for SOME guest-reachable access, and (b) pass all 22 rows.  For each: the exact edit, the
reachable access it breaks (instruction, width, port, lane), and why every one of the 22 rows stays green.  In
particular, test the width axis: `ofs = (relative_addr & 3) % len' partitions len into {1}, {2}, {3}, {>= 4}, and the
rows use len 1 (LDBU/STB) and len 4 (LDL/STL) only.  Is a variant keyed on `len == 2' (LDWU/STW) or `len == 3' (an
unaligned access that memory_rw splits at a 1 KB boundary) reachable by a guest, and does any row see it?

## TASK 2 -- ROW VALIDITY, citing file:line

For each CONTROL row (`live`, `flat lsr`, `ram plant`, `ptbr miss`, `ptbr sent`, `pte2/3 todo`, `sp lsr ans`): what
does it print when the thing it guards is FALSE?  Any row whose channel cannot vary under its named mutant is
vacuous -- name it.

## TASK 3 -- THE FIX ITSELF

Does `CACHE_NONE | PHYSICAL` change anything else `memory_rw` does for this address (the RAM path's
update_translation_table, watchpoints, the #95 pre-zero)?  With PHYSICAL, can the inner read still fail, and what does
the guest then see (the read arm ignores the inner return value)?

**If you found anything else while verifying a claim, say so briefly.  Mark every claim MEASURED, READ (file:line) or
INFERRED.**
